reasoning_over_luck.evidence:
WHAT THERE IS TO INFER: the full deck composition is known and each seat knows its own 10 cards. The 6 unseen cards in each band are split between two opponents. Up piles are public, and every play and pickup is public, so card counting steadily narrows opponents' hands. For example, if two 9s are gone and you hold none, a 9 on top forces an opponent without C into a PICK UP.

DECISIONS:
- which 3 cards go up (keep a high card or C for the end game);
- when to spend the single C;
- whether to complete a run wipe or leave a high top for the next seat, who may be known to lack the rank or have spent C;
- whether to shed pairs now or keep them.

SOURCES OF CHANCE: the band-balanced deal limits luck to rank clustering. Holding three copies of a rank gives a free self-wipe. A heavily clustered hand of 9s helps the end game. I estimate chance at about 30-35% of outcomes, plus the seat-order effect noted under balanced. With all seats playing well, the winner still depends on pile management and timing of C, not only the deal. A reasoner who counts cards beats always-first or random play clearly. The deal adds variety but does not decide equal-skill games on its own.
